        </SdkVersion>
        <Options>
            <Main applyHouseholdOperatingConditions="false" applyReducedOperatingConditions="false" atypicalElectricalLoads="false" waterConservation="false" referenceHouse="false">
                <Vermiculite code="1">
                    <English>Unknown</English>
                    <French>Inconnu</French>
                </Vermiculite>
            </Main>
            <RURComments xml:space="preserve">
                <airSeal>There were a few areas that I had noticed that showed significant air leakage. They are listed below:



Around the older windows.



Around the bath room fan.



The area at the top of the basement stairs that had some exposed insulation.



Around the basement door.



Replacing the older windows, or at least replacing the older hinged and sliding windows would decrease air leakage into the home. Picture windows are not very leaky but do not have significant insulating value.



The bathroom fan cover could be removed and have some spray foam and silicon inserted around the fan to decrease air leakage.



The area at the top of the stairs needs to be assessed by a carpenter to decide the best plan of action for that area. Having properly insulated and air sealed wall and vapor barrier will help with this area. 



The basement door may need a new door sweep or may just need some new weather stripping.</airSeal>
                <mainWalls>Upgrade the rmain walls above grade with an additional 2" of rigid board foram (R-10) value. This will be the same as the front wall you had already revovated. The new r-value of the walls will be R-21 compared to the R-11 they currently are. There is a rebate available from the Energy Solutions Centre for $0.50/square foot of insulation added with a value between R-10 and R-19. </mainWalls>
                <ceilings>I assumed there is only R-20 batt insulation in the attic. The insulation should be upgraded with R-20 blown in cellulose over top of the existing insulation. There are rebates available from the Energy Solutions Centre for $2.50/square foot of insulation added with a value of R-20 or higher. Contact the Energy Solutions Centre for more information on upgrading and to proper procedures to reveive the rebates.</ceilings>
                <foundation>The foundation walls should have 2" of rigid board added to the exterior of the walls. The exterior on the front and sides of the house would need to have insulation go down 2.5 feet. The back side of the house where it is higher above grade should have the full height off the wall upgraded. The rebate for adding R-10 to a foundation wall is $0.50/square foot. Please contact the Energy Solutions Centre for more information about the rebates.



The header area of the foundation wall could be upgraded as well. You could take out the existing R-12 batt and fill it with 3" of medium density spray foam. This will help with air leakage and increase the insulation value</foundation>
                <windows>The windows that are older should be replaced with Energy Start windows rated  for climate zone 3. There are rebates available for $100/window from the Energy Solutions Centre for up to 10 windows.</windows>
                <VentilationSystem>If you decide to get new windows, and do some upgrades on your walls to make your home more energy efficient  and tighter than you may want to consider installing a HRV in the future. This will provide the home with proper ventilation and will increase air quality in the home. There is a $500 rebate available from the Energy Solutions Centre for new HRVs installed. Contact the ESC more more information on this.</VentilationSystem>
                <HotWaterSystem>There is potential for a drain water heat recovery system. This would take hot water from a shower drain and preheat the cold water entering the hot water tank. The cold water line to the tank would wrap around the drain and then enter the hot water tank. This system can save electrcity from being used to heat the hot water. There is a $150 rebate for drain water heat recovery systems that are installed. Check with Energy Solutions Centre for more information. A certified  plumber would need to install this.</HotWaterSystem>
            </RURComments>
        </Options>
